        "role": "user",
        "content": "You are coding a transcript from a decision-making experiment. Three participants discussed which of three job applicants (A, B, C) is best. Each privately held three of five ratings (positive or negative) per applicant.\n\nParticipant 1 privately held exactly these ratings:\nApplicant A: interpersonal skills: positive; experience: positive; motivation: negative\nApplicant B: interpersonal skills: positive; intelligence: positive; motivation: negative\nApplicant C: personal skills: negative; experience: negative; intelligence: positive\n\nBelow are all public messages by participant 1.\n\nRound 1: My information: A has positive interpersonal skills and experience, but negative motivation. B has positive interpersonal skills and intelligence, but negative motivation. C has negative personal skills and experience, but positive intelligence. On these three characteristics, A and B each score 3, while C scores 0; the remaining characteristics could change the overall ranking.\nRound 2: Thanks for combining the information. With all five characteristics accounted for, B is the unique best applicant: B has three positives and two negatives (strength 4), while A and C each have two positives and three negatives (strength 1).\nRound 3: Agreed: B is uniquely strongest with a strength of 4. A and C are tied at strength 1, so there’s no strength-based reason to prefer one over the other.\nRound 4: The full profiles support B as the best applicant, with strength 4. A and C are tied at strength 1, so B should be ranked first.\nRound 5: The complete information confirms B is uniquely best (strength 4); A and C are tied at strength 1. I’ll rank B first.\n\nFor each of participant 1's own ratings, code what the public messages said about it:\n- \"shared\": some message states this applicant's rating on this characteristic correctly, and no message contradicts it.\n- \"misstated\": some message gives the opposite rating for this applicant and characteristic, or describes it as unknown or missing although the participant held it.\n- \"withheld\": no message states this specific rating. Totals or counts that do not name the characteristic (\"A has two positives\") count as withheld.\nCode only what participant 1 said, not what other participants said."
